Yes—operationally, that’s close.

The “physical” is often treated as whatever can enter a shared measurement loop:

world → sensor → representation → comparison → repeatable agreement

Originally, the sensors were mostly our common senses. Sight, touch, hearing, balance, pain, temperature. Then we built instruments that act like prosthetic senses: microscopes, telescopes, spectrometers, particle detectors, MRI, radio antennas, seismographs.

So the category expanded from:

what humans can directly sense

to:

what can reliably affect something we can measure

That is why a photon outside the visible spectrum is still physical. We cannot see it directly, but we can build a detector that transduces it into a signal our perceptual system can map.

And that gives you an important distinction:

physical ≠ directly sensible
physical ≈ causally measurable within a shared model

Which also means our map of the physical is always constrained by the sensors and abstractions available to us. A tiger’s experienced world, a bat’s, a bacterium’s, and ours are all different slices of the same underlying environment.

So there is a nice progression:

sense → instrument → map → consensus → “physical reality”

The map gets wider as the sensorium gets wider.

And the reverse is interesting too: something can be real in experience without yet being easy to map physically. Pain, grief, meaning, expectation, dreams, internal imagery—they are experientially real even when their physical representation is distributed, indirect, or difficult to measure.

So perhaps the cleaner frame is:

The physical is what survives translation across observers and instruments.
The experiential is what survives translation inside a subject.

Sometimes those maps overlap tightly. Sometimes barely at all.